# CPU core-rail regulators for MT6797 (B-40, issue #55).
#
# The A72 cluster's supply is VPROC2, and the vendor drives it through a Dialog
# DA9214 dual buck at 7-bit 0x68 on I2C bus 6
# (drivers/misc/mediatek/power/mt6797/da9214.c). Mainline's da9211 driver covers
# DA9214 explicitly.
#
# BUILT AS A MODULE ON PURPOSE, not built in.
#
# The regulator core disables regulators nothing has claimed, in a sweep that
# runs once at late_initcall. A driver that registers after that sweep is not
# swept. Since it is not yet established which of the DA9214's two bucks is
# live — one of them is very probably the rail the running A53 clusters depend
# on — a built-in driver would mean the first boot with a DA9214 node is also
# the first boot that could switch off the CPUs' own supply, with the panel
# going with them.
#
# As a module nothing binds until `modprobe da9211`, which makes identifying
# the two bucks a deliberate, observed step rather than something that happens
# during boot while nobody is looking.
# CHANGED TO =y on 2026-08-22, once cpufreq needed it.
#
# The reasoning above was right while the identity of the two bucks was
# unknown. It is known now, measured on #46/#47: BUCKA is the live A53 rail at
# 1000 mV, BUCKB is VPROC2 and is off. BUCKA carries regulator-always-on, so
# the late_initcall sweep leaves it alone whenever it binds.
#
# It has to be built in because mediatek-cpufreq resolves proc-supply during
# boot; a module loaded from the rootfs afterwards is too late, and cpufreq
# would have given up on the deferred probe by then.
CONFIG_REGULATOR_DA9211=y
